With Mutual Approval

CHAPTER III
FORMS OF VIOLENCE
Article 4
Forms of violence against women and children can be in the form of:
a. physical violence;
b. psychological violence;
c. sexual violence;
d. neglect; and
e. exploitation.

Article 8
Neglect as referred to in Article 4 letter d is caused by:
a. acts that result in the failure to fulfill the needs of children properly, whether physical, mental, spiritual or social, committed by parents, guardians, or other parties responsible for care;
b. acts of deliberately neglecting to maintain, care for, or look after children as appropriate, committed by parents, guardians or any other party responsible for their care;
c. acts of neglecting a person within the scope of one's household, whereas according to the law applicable to him or her or due to an agreement or contract he or she is obliged to provide livelihood, care, or maintenance to that person; and/or
d. acts that result in economic dependency by means of restricting and/or prohibiting proper work inside or outside the home so that the victim is under the control of that person.

6.7.text:
Article 21
(1) To prevent the occurrence of violence against women and children, the government conducts empowerment and awareness raising to families, parents and the community by providing information, guidance and/or counseling.
(2) In addition to empowerment and awareness raising as referred to in paragraph (1), the government undertakes efforts as follows:
a. increasing the quantity and quality of education both formal and non-formal and informal;
b. opening accessibility to obtain education, training, funding, income enhancement and social services;
c. opening employment opportunities for women;
d. building community participation and concern to implement prevention and protection of women and children from violence;
e. building and providing a complete and easily accessible information system;
f. building networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, universities and various non-governmental organizations that work and/or care for women and children; and
g. opening complaint posts for the protection of women and children from violence.

Article 3
(1) The objectives of implementing protection for women and children victims of violence are to:
a. prevent violence against women and children including human trafficking;
b. eliminate all forms of violence and exploitation against women and children;
c. protect and provide a sense of security for women and children;
d. provide services to women and children victims of violence, reporters, and witnesses; and
e. facilitate and mediate domestic disputes to realize the integrity of a harmonious and prosperous household.
(2) The objectives of implementing protection for women and children victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), include aspects of:
a. prevention;
b. services and assistance;
c. reunification; and
d. empowerment.

Article 22
(1) Prevention of the occurrence of violence against women and children as referred to in Article 21, shall be implemented by regional apparatus whose duties and functions are in the fields of:
a. social affairs;
b. health;
c. education;
d. manpower;
e. women's empowerment and child protection;
f. mental and spiritual; and
g. public peace and order.
(2) Prevention of violence by regional apparatus as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be implemented in an integrated and continuous manner based on the Regional Action Plan.

Article 13
(1) The Regional Government shall implement efforts to protect women and children victims of violence through:
a. implementing policies for the protection of women and children victims of violence as applied by the government;
b. establishing policies, programs, and activities for the protection of women and children victims of violence;
c. the establishment of PPT;
d. ensuring the provision of ease of access to services for victims;
e. pursuing effectiveness and efficiency for the victim recovery process; and
f. pursuing the creation of cooperation and coordination in victim recovery efforts.
(2) In order to implement the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b, the regional government shall establish action programs and activities for the protection of women and children in 1 (one) Regional Action Plan as a basis for regional apparatus in implementing protection for women and children victims of violence.
(3) The Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2) constitutes part of the Regional Medium-Term Development Plan (RPJMD).
(4) The Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be further regulated by a Mayor Regulation.

